<body>

<h1>DBMS DA3: Dynamic Web Application with JDBC Connectivity</h1>
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>GitHub Repository:</strong> <a href="https://github.com/AviDhandhania/dbms-da3">https://github.com/AviDhandhania/dbms-da3</a></p>

<h2>1. Introduction</h2>
<p>This project is a dynamic Database Management System (DBMS) web application that provides a fully functional Frontend, Backend, and Database Connectivity. The system allows users to dynamically manage Oracle database tables (DDL), manipulate data within those tables (DML), and run arbitrary SQL queries directly from a web interface.</p>

<h2>2. Frontend Features and Screenshots</h2>
<p>The frontend is built using standard HTML5, CSS3, and JavaScript (Fetch API). It features a modern dashboard UI designed to act as a lightweight database client.</p>

<h3>2.1. The Table Manager</h3>
<p>The Table Manager allows users to dynamically fetch a list of all tables in the database, select a table, and view its contents. Users can dynamically add new rows via auto-generated forms, delete rows, or drop the table entirely.</p>
<div class="screenshot-placeholder">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64," style="max-width: 100%; border: 1px solid #ddd; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
</div>

<h3>2.2. Table Creation (DDL)</h3>
<p>By clicking "New Table", users are presented with a UI to define a new table name and its schema, which executes a CREATE TABLE statement in the backend.</p>
<div class="screenshot-placeholder">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64," style="max-width: 100%; border: 1px solid #ddd; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
</div>

<h3>2.3. SQL Query Runner</h3>
<p>This feature allows advanced users to write and execute any raw SQL query. It features a query history dropdown to recall previously run queries and provides the ability to export the resulting dataset to a CSV file.</p>
<div class="screenshot-placeholder">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64," style="max-width: 100%; border: 1px solid #ddd; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
</div>

<h2>3. Frontend Code Explanation</h2>
<p>The frontend is contained entirely within <code>index.html</code>. It uses the Fetch API to make asynchronous HTTP requests to the backend.</p>
<pre>
// Example: Executing a dynamic SQL query from the frontend
async function executeSQL(query) {
    const response = await fetch('http://localhost:8080/api/query', {
        method: 'POST',
        headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded' },
        body: `query=${encodeURIComponent(query)}`
    });
    return response.json();
}
</pre>
<p>This function forms the backbone of the UI. Whether the user is dropping a table, inserting a row, or running a raw query, the frontend simply constructs the appropriate SQL string and sends it to the backend via this <code>executeSQL</code> function.</p>

<h2>4. Backend Code Explanation</h2>
<p>The backend is written in plain Java (<code>BackendCRUD.java</code>) utilizing the built-in <code>com.sun.net.httpserver.HttpServer</code>. It avoids heavy frameworks and provides a fast, lightweight REST API.</p>
<pre>
// Starting the HTTP Server
HttpServer server = HttpServer.create(new InetSocketAddress(8080), 0);
server.createContext("/api/users", new ApiHandler());
server.createContext("/api/query", new QueryHandler());
server.setExecutor(null);
server.start();
</pre>
<p>The <code>QueryHandler</code> receives POST requests, decodes the SQL query string, and passes it to the JDBC driver. If the query returns a <code>ResultSet</code> (like a SELECT statement), it dynamically parses the metadata to count the columns and constructs a JSON array of objects to send back to the frontend. If it is a DDL/DML statement, it executes it and returns the affected row count or success message.</p>

<h2>5. JDBC Connectivity Explanation</h2>
<p>The database connection is established using the Oracle Thin JDBC driver (<code>ojdbc6.jar</code>). </p>
<pre>
// Database Credentials
static final String DB_URL = "jdbc:oracle:thin:@//localhost:1521/XE";
static final String USER = "system";
static final String PASS = "oracle";

// Registering Driver and Connecting
Class.forName("oracle.jdbc.driver.OracleDriver");
Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection(DB_URL, USER, PASS);
</pre>
<p>The connection leverages the <code>java.sql.Connection</code> and <code>java.sql.DriverManager</code> classes. The application creates a connection per request inside a try-with-resources block to ensure the connection is automatically closed after the HTTP request is served, preventing resource leaks.</p>
<pre>
// Dynamic query execution block
try (Statement stmt = conn.createStatement()) {
    boolean isResultSet = stmt.execute(query);
    if (isResultSet) {
        try (ResultSet rs = stmt.getResultSet()) {
            ResultSetMetaData rsmd = rs.getMetaData();
            // Parse ResultSet to JSON...
        }
    }
}
</pre>
<p>The <code>Statement.execute(query)</code> method is heavily utilized because it can run ANY type of SQL statement dynamically. It returns a boolean indicating whether the first result is a <code>ResultSet</code> (true) or an update count (false).</p>

